    <semantic:process id="_1ec9a736-68af-491a-86d0-e9d2b8631006" name="Page 1" triso:readOnly="false" triso:collectEnds="true" isClosed="false">
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_c1604c92-5084-4832-87e0-9477a08be082">
            <semantic:text>tokens</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_b36be970-9071-400b-bf18-e762d84f9b4e">
            <semantic:text>Define Message flow (intro) in BPMN terms.
Identify when to apply Message flow (intro).
Model a 4-6 step example using Message flow (intro) correctly.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_492c10d9-4d83-4f30-8712-b64d0d0962af">
            <semantic:text>Today's concept: Message flow (intro). In a e-sports tournament bracket admin scenario, ask: What starts the flow, where can it split, how does it rejoin, and what ends it? Name tasks verb-object and avoid crossing lines. If a decision is needed, pick the gateway that matches the semantics. If data crosses pools, use a message flow, not a sequence flow. Keep one clear idea per diagram. Here's the one-sentence way to remember it: make it obvious, and test it with token animation if your tool supports it. Try modeling it in under 60 seconds.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
    </semantic:process>
